00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
unlv health schools are teaming up for a  poverty simulation the goal is to build  empathy and expose students to the  impacts of poverty here in our community  mary jane belliesa shares how students  are reacting as they transition into  their medical careers  this poverty simulation is an  interactive simulation teaching medical  dental and nursing students what it's  like for those living near or on the  poverty line students will encounter  obstacles as they navigate through daily  tasks like managing transportation needs  or applying for jobs organizer mini wood  says this exercise will help teach  students how to serve and provide  quality care to low-income individuals  poverty is not an issue of individual  failure or individual responsibility but  it's a systemic and structural issue  they can you know understand how all of  our structures and systems interact  together to create tremendous barriers  and obstacles for people who are trying  to get out of poverty dental student  yulin lee says there is a constant  struggle to meet basic needs at first it  seemed very like superficial easy but  now that i'm actually  in the situation and see how the family  is like trying to figure out  how to get to places how to pay for like  mortgage and stuff kind of kind of hit  me i think those were things that i was  very oblivious to honestly nursing  student silo mamaclay is given the role  of an elderly woman citing financial  insecurity and neglect as big issues  among seniors it's our job to be  compassionate and be empathetic towards  people you never really know who you're  going to be taking care of whether  they're homeless elderly being abused if  they have no insurance so  this is really eye-opening reporting  from the paul mcdermott education center  at unlv mary jane vallieza live local  now
 

